UID:9180cd8eaa37a6f9@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260807T001402Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Registration is Open for the 2026 MaineCare P
 rovider Conference
DESCRIPTION:Maine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Ma
 ine.gov\nDHHS\n→\nOffice of MaineCare Services (OMS)\n→\nProvider Reso
 urces\n→\n	 \n			Registration is Open for the 2026 MaineCare Provider Co
 nference\nRegistration is Open for the 2026 MaineCare Provider Conference\
 nAug 06\, 2026\nJoin colleagues from across Maine for the inaugural\n2026 
 MaineCare Provider Conference\n—a dynamic day of learning\, collaboratio
 n\, and connection. Centered on the theme\n"Stronger Together: Navigating 
 Change Through Collaboration\,"\nthis conference brings together providers
  from across the MaineCare community to share innovative practices\, explo
 re emerging challenges\, and strengthen partnerships that improve care for
  the people we serve.\nThis conference is made possible through support fr
 om Maine's Rural Health Transformation Program (RHTP)\, a federal initiati
 ve that helps strengthen rural healthcare by improving access to care\, su
 pporting the healthcare workforce\, modernizing healthcare systems\, and a
 dvancing innovative models of care across the state. The conference reflec
 ts these goals by creating opportunities for providers to learn from one a
 nother\, build stronger partnerships\, and share strategies that improve c
 are for MaineCare members.\nDesigned for professionals across primary care
 \, behavioral health\, oral health\, community support services\, and othe
 r disciplines\, the conference features interactive presentations\, practi
 cal strategies\, and real-world solutions that participants can immediatel
 y apply in their work. Whether you're looking to expand your knowledge\, e
 xchange ideas with peers\, or build new connections across Maine's healthc
 are and social service systems\, this conference offers valuable opportuni
 ties to learn from one another and shape the future of care.\nThe conferen
 ce welcomes participants in all roles with MaineCare provider organization
 s\n\, including:\nClinicians\nAdministrators\nCare coordinators\nProgram s
 taff\nQuality improvement\nprofessionals\nOther team members who support M
 aine\nhttps://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/registra
 tion-open-2026-mainecare-provider-conference-2026-08-06
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/registrati
 on-open-2026-mainecare-provider-conference-2026-08-06
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:6774c3d0eb016a3d@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260807T001402Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Section 18\, Home and Community Based Service
 s for Adults with Brain Injury Rate: Additional Services Identified for Re
 moval of Service Provider Tax (SPT) CR 141031
DESCRIPTION:Maine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Ma
 ine.gov\nDHHS\n→\nOffice of MaineCare Services (OMS)\n→\nProvider Reso
 urces\n→\n	 \n			Section 18\, Home and Community Based Services for Adul
 ts with Brain Injury Rate: Additional Services Identified for Removal of S
 ervice Provider Tax (SPT) CR 141031\nSection 18\, Home and Community Based
  Services for Adults with Brain Injury Rate: Additional Services Identifie
 d for Removal of Service Provider Tax (SPT) CR 141031\nAug 06\, 2026\nEffe
 ctive August 1\, 2026\, MaineCare\nupdated\ncertain Section 18 reimburseme
 nt rates to reflect the removal of the SPT\, in alignment with P.L. 2023\
 , Chapter 412\, Part XXX. Please see our September 2024\ne-message\nfor mo
 re information.\nThe services listed below were mistakenly overlooked when
  the Department\noriginally identified\ndocumented add-on amounts associat
 ed with the SPT.\nProcedure Code\nModifier\nCode Description\nUnit of Serv
 ice\nRate Ending 7/31/2026\nRate Effective 8/1/2026\nT2016\nU9 TG\nHome Su
 pport (Residential Habilitation) Level III - Increased Neurobehavioral\, 2
 -4 Beds\nPer Diem\n$665.04\n$627.40\nT2016\nU9 TG TT\nHome Support (Reside
 ntial Habilitation) Level III - Increased Neurobehavioral\, 5-8 Beds\nPer 
 Diem\n$559.55\n$527.87\nThe full\nfee schedule\nis available on the Rate S
 etting page of the Health PAS Online Portal.\nAdjustment Billing Guidance 
 for Dates of Service August 1\, 2026 – August 6\, 2026\nProviders are re
 sponsible for adjusting claims submitted with charges\nbelow\nthe new rate
 s. Per policy\, and to avoid timely filing denials\, adjustments must be s
 ubmitted within 120 days of the most recent claim payment date.\nFor quest
 ions\, please contact your\nProvider Relations Specialist\n.\nhttps://www.
 maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/section-18-home-and-commun
 ity-based-services-adults-brain-injury-rate-additional-services
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/section-18
 -home-and-community-based-services-adults-brain-injury-rate-additional-ser
 vices
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:79e3d8a3e19d6408@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260807T001402Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Addition of the TH Modifier to Support Nation
 al Maternity Care Coding and Reimbursement Changes Effective September 1\,
  2026
DESCRIPTION:Maine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Ma
 ine.gov\nDHHS\n→\nOffice of MaineCare Services (OMS)\n→\nProvider Reso
 urces\n→\n	 \n			Addition of the TH Modifier to Support National Materni
 ty Care Coding and Reimbursement Changes Effective September 1\, 2026\nAdd
 ition of the TH Modifier to Support National Maternity Care Coding and Rei
 mbursement Changes Effective September 1\, 2026\nAug 06\, 2026\nThe\nAmeri
 can Medical Association (AMA) announced maternity care services code chan
 ges\nthat will be effective January 1\, 2027. These changes include the re
 moval of\n17\nbilling codes\, the addition of 12 billing codes\, and revis
 ions to 6 billing codes to reflect modern obstetric practices\, allow for 
 more accurate and transparent reporting\, and to create a data foundation 
 to better attribute maternal care and support the complexities of team-bas
 ed care throughout a patient's journey.\nIn preparation for these changes\
 , MaineCare is also making changes to coding requirements for maternity ca
 re services\, including the transition to non-global and non-bundled care 
 coding and reimbursement. To ensure consistent reimbursement MaineCare is 
 directing providers to take the following actions no later than prior to S
 eptember 1\, 2026:\nAdjust maternity care visit billing from global or bun
 dled service codes to Evaluation and Management (E/M) service codes based 
 on each member’s estimated due date and the anticipated number of visits
  for the remainder of their antepartum period in accordance with\nAMA Ante
 partum Transition Reporting guidance\;\n.\nAppend the TH modifier for all 
 maternity care E/M service codes on your claim to identify the visit as a 
 maternity care service\;\nTransition from total-visit tracking to individu
 al encounter documentation\, as each prenatal and postpartum visit must no
 w independently support standard E/M complexity or time rules\; and\nMonit
 or professional guidance from organizations like the American College of O
 bstetrics and Gynecology and the AMA to prepare billers and systems\nhttps
 ://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/addition-th-modifie
 r-support-national-maternity-care-coding-and-reimbursement-changes
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/addition-t
 h-modifier-support-national-maternity-care-coding-and-reimbursement-change
 s
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:b804b6118caafd21@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260807T001402Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Section 65 Requirement for Monthly Average of
  Three Contacts per Week for Children’s Assertive Community Treatment (A
 CT) and Home and Community Based Treatment (HCT)
DESCRIPTION:Maine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Ma
 ine.gov\nDHHS\n→\nOffice of MaineCare Services (OMS)\n→\nProvider Reso
 urces\n→\n	 \n			Section 65 Requirement for Monthly Average of Three Con
 tacts per Week for Children’s Assertive Community Treatment (ACT) and Ho
 me and Community Based Treatment (HCT)\nSection 65 Requirement for Monthly
  Average of Three Contacts per Week for Children’s Assertive Community T
 reatment (ACT) and Home and Community Based Treatment (HCT)\nAug 06\, 2026
 \nThe Section 65 rule adopted on April 28\, 2026\, requires that ACT and H
 CT treatment teams provide a monthly average of three contacts per week\, 
 at least one  of which is a clinical intervention with a Clinician. See o
 ur\nFrequently Asked Questions document\nfor billing guidance related to t
 hese requirements.\nhttps://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bull
 etins/section-65-requirement-monthly-average-three-contacts-week-childrens
 -assertive-community
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/section-65
 -requirement-monthly-average-three-contacts-week-childrens-assertive-commu
 nity
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c2bdd98e2e202891@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260806T143250Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine DEP Issues Air Quality Alert as Particl
 e Pollution Levels Predicted to Escalate to Unhealthy Ranges
DESCRIPTION:Maine DEP Issues Air Quality Alert as Particle Pollution Level
 s Predicted to Escalate to Unhealthy Ranges\nMaine Department of Environme
 ntal Protection sent this bulletin at 07/17/2026 03:23 PM EDT\n--- PRESS R
 ELEASE ---\nFor Immediate Release\nMaine DEP Issues Air Quality Alert as P
 article Pollution Levels Predicted to Escalate to Unhealthy Ranges\nAUGUST
 A\, July 17\, 2026\n— The Maine Department of Environmental Protection w
 arns that ground-level particle pollution concentrations are forecasted to
  reach the ‘Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups’ (USG) level this Saturday 
 due to wildfire smoke drifting in from Manitoba and Ontario\, Canada.\nThe
  smoke plume is expected to move back over Maine during the day on Saturda
 y\, with air quality measured by a 24-hour average forecasted at the USG l
 evel. Some hours may experience particle pollution levels even higher than
  this range. Residents are encouraged to follow health recommendations tha
 t suit their individual needs.\nSmoke conditions may improve by midday Sun
 day\, but due to the unpredictable nature of wildfire smoke\, the Maine De
 partment of Environmental Protection advises checking their website or cal
 ling the toll-free Air Quality Hotline at 800-223-1196 for the latest upda
 tes throughout the weekend.\nElevated particle pollution levels can affect
  sensitive groups such as children\, older adults\, outdoor workers\, and 
 individuals with respiratory or heart conditions including asthma\, bronch
 itis\, and COPD. Symptoms may include coughing\, shortness of breath\, thr
 oat irritation\, and mild chest pain. Even healthy adults engaging in outd
 oor physical activity might notice these effects.\nSome actions you can ta
 ke to protect your health during such periods include:\nAvoiding strenuous
  outdoor activity\nClosing windows and circulating indoor air with a fan o
 r air conditioner\nAsthmatics should keep their quick-relief medications a
 nd action plan handy\nAdditional health information may be found on the fo
 llowing websites:\nMaine CDC asthma prevention an\nhttps://content.govdeli
 very.com/accounts/MEDEP/bulletins/420fa43
URL:https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts/MEDEP/bulletins/420fa43
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
